Interpretation: When adding an element at position index, elements from index to oldSize-1 shift right by one. So after operation, for each i in [index, oldSize-1] (old indices), the element at position i+1 after addition equals the old element at position i. Also need to consider null handling: "either null if the old element at this position was null, or it equals the old element at this position". That's essentially same as equality, but they emphasize null case. In Java, equality with .equals cannot be called on null. So we need to express: either old element is null and new element is null, or old element is non-null and equals new element. So condition: (\old(this.get(i)) == null && this.get(i+1) == null) || (\old(this.get(i)) != null && this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i)))).

But we also need premise: index between 0 and initial size inclusive. The initial size is old size: \old(this.size()).

So premise: 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()).

Now quantifier: for all int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); the property holds.

Thus assert: index>=0 && index<=\old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); ( (\old(this.get(i)) == null && this.get(i+1) == null) || (\old(this.get(i)) != null && this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i)))) );

